{"author":"Joe Oakhart","author_email":"joeoakhart@navapbc.com","data":{"body":"CONCURRENCY LIMIT, MEASURED THE HARD WAY. Six supervisor lanes were run in parallel at ~23:00Z. 61 voids followed within roughly three hours, against 20 for the whole campaign before that. Two distinct failure modes, both concurrency-induced, neither a defect in the arm under test: mini returned provider InternalServerError with COST 0.0 and CALLS 1 (nothing billed, but the draw is burned); ce exited 8 'INSTRUMENT LOST' with no per-subagent transcript written and the -p background-wait ceiling firing, which its own runner refuses to admit as a draw. gstack and sp5 failed at comparable wall times. The prior working configuration was two lanes. Recommended ceiling: two concurrent lanes, and note that rebar additionally serialises against itself via the shared checkout's plan-review cache guard, so a second rebar dispatch refuses while one is live. Lanes were reduced to zero at the end of this session; two orphaned gstack runners were left to finish and need  afterwards to record their terminal state. The voids from this window are recorded and the affected draws are owed again -- they are not lost, but they consumed wall time and, for the non-mini arms, real spend."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789773630516635001,"uuid":"0a80a037-e623-4978-9800-338c7916270d"}